Subject: [PATCH] metal: keep sampled textures across draws, keyed by what their decode reads

Every sampled bind on the Metal rail was a fresh MTLTexture: the guest
texture's rows read, every texel decoded, the result uploaded, per draw.
Measured on an iPhone running a macOS guest at its login window: 48 binds a
second moved 1.3 GB (the wallpaper, redrawn), with metal_sampled_load_us at
436 ms and metal_sampled_tex_upload_us at 185 ms of every second — most of a
host core, on a host whose two fast cores run the guest's emulated CPUs.

This device does not see the guest CPU's writes to texture memory, so nothing
can know a texture is unchanged without reading it. For the plain path of
load_sampled_rgba (level 0, own format; not a buffer texture, a
mapper-ref-texture surface or a view — the same checks in the same order), the
rows the decode would read are read through the same translation and hashed
with every other input of the decode: geometry, pitch, both formats. A key
kept by backend::metal::sampled_cache binds the texture that decode produced;
a miss decodes as before and keeps the result. A hit saves the decode and the
upload; the read is paid either way.

The cache is a bound in the sense AGENTS.md allows: every entry derives from
guest pages that are still there, and an evicted one costs a decode. 160 MB on
iOS, 256 MB elsewhere, least recently used first; a texture larger than the
whole bound is not kept; 160 MB on iOS holds the 6016x6016 login wallpaper (138 MiB), which 96 MB refused on every bind. Hits, misses and evictions are store routes.

Tested: the key follows the guest's bytes (equal when unchanged, including
across padding the decode never reads; different after one texel is
rewritten) and reports the geometry the plain load does; the hash does not
depend on how its input is split. The Metal draw suite passes through the
cache.
